<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Cyra app privacy manifest (App target).

  Scope: everything this app sends off the device, judged from src/lib/api.js (every
  fetch to the backend), src/lib/native.js and ios/App/App/CyraAuthPlugin.swift (the
  sign-in and Oura/Terra connect windows, which open the backend's /api/oauth/:id/start and
  /api/integrations/oura/start pages, or Terra's widget, as browser navigations and come back
  through the backend's /callback and /done pages), src/App.jsx, src/lib/pulse.js,
  src/lib/wearables.js, src/lib/notifications.js, src/lib/storage.js, src/lib/backup.js,
  src/lib/links.js, src/screens/ReportScreen.jsx, src/main.jsx and src/styles.js, and from
  what backend/src/modules (ai, pulse, integrations, oauth, push) do with it. Apple's definition of "collect": transmitting data off the device in a way
  that allows you and/or your third-party partners to access it for longer than needed to
  service the request in real time. Every flow is either declared below as a collected
  data type or listed here as not collected, with the reason.

  The health log is stored only on the device. Without any opt-in, Cyra sends one request at
  each launch, GET /api/pulse, which carries no life stage, health data or identifier (see
  NOT collected), plus the GET /api/oauth/providers and /api/integrations/sources checks when
  she taps a sign-in or Connect button (see NOT collected). Everything else Cyra sends goes
  only after the person turns that feature on (the user-directed hand-offs, the encrypted
  backup and the doctor summary are listed under NOT collected below):
  - "Share anonymous weekly counts": life stage group plus yes/no event flags, at most once
    a day, with no token or identifier (POST /api/pulse/tally).
  - "Also ask Cyra's AI": the typed question only, and only when the on-device library has
    no answer (POST /api/ai/ask). The backend forwards it to Anthropic.
  - Oura: the backend relays readings from Oura in real time and stores nothing.
  - Fitbit, Garmin, Whoop through Terra: readings wait in the backend's memory (never on
    disk, deleted when collected or after 7 days), in a mailbox keyed by a one-way hash of a
    secret key that is kept on this device and in any encrypted backup the user exports. The
    device sends the key only in request bodies, and the backend uses it for that request and
    never stores it.
  - Social sign-in: email (only when Apple or Google says it is verified) and name, held for
    a 5-minute hand-off that a timer enforces.

  NOT collected, so not declared:
  - Apple Health data read by CyraHealthPlugin. It is read and stored on this device
    (Library/NoCloud, marked excluded from backup) and is never sent to Cyra's servers or
    anyone else; the plugin makes no network calls. It leaves the device only inside the
    passphrase-encrypted backup file, if the user exports one.
  - The encrypted backup: a file the user exports and keeps wherever she chooses,
    encrypted on the device with a key derived from a passphrase only she holds. It never
    goes to a Cyra server.
  - The doctor summary (Report > Open in email / Copy email): a last-30-days summary (how
    many of those days she logged; up to four symptoms with their day counts and
    moderate-to-strong counts; cycle lengths and their spread; in perimenopause, how much
    more likely hot flashes were after poorly rated nights), with her saved name, if any, in
    the subject. Cyra hands it to her own mail app as an unsent draft or puts it on her
    clipboard. Cyra sends nothing itself and its servers never receive it. What happens to it
    next depends on her mail app and clipboard: some mail apps save an open draft to the mail
    provider's server before she sends it, and the system clipboard can reach her other
    devices (Universal Clipboard) or apps allowed to read it.
  - The weekly counts read, GET /api/pulse: it carries no life stage and no identifier;
    every device gets the same answer (all three life stages, last closed week), and Cyra's
    backend code keeps nothing from the request (the host's request log is declared under
    Product interaction).
  - The welcome sentence and the routing to the single life-stage experience: both are
    made on the device, and nothing is sent.
  - Reminders. The phone build schedules local notifications on the device; the Web Push
    subscription in backend/src/modules/push is used only by the web build.
  - Fonts. They are bundled with the app (src/main.jsx imports @fontsource-variable), so
    no font server is contacted.
  - Sign-in and connect windows. They show Apple's, Google's, Facebook's, Oura's or Terra's
    own pages (and, inside Terra's, Fitbit's, Garmin's or Whoop's) in an ephemeral
    ASWebAuthenticationSession; what those pages collect falls under that company's own
    policy. What they send back to Cyra's backend is used only for that request: the OAuth
    callback reads the provider's account id, tokens and id_token claims (and Facebook's
    email) and drops them once it has taken the name and the verified email, and the done
    page ignores the user_id, reference_id and anything else Terra adds to its address.
    Those addresses reach the hosting provider like any request address. What the backend
    keeps longer than the request is declared below (Email address, Name, User ID, Other
    data types).
  - Requests that carry nothing beyond what every request carries (IP address, user
    agent): GET /api/oauth/providers and GET /api/integrations/sources (which sign-in and
    wearable services the server offers), and opening Cyra's Terms or Privacy Policy, which
    shows the published address in a browser view (Capacitor Browser) with nothing from
    Cyra added. (Cyra's code keeps nothing from these. The backend host's request logs for
    the two GET calls are declared below under Product interaction. The policy pages are
    served from whatever address VITE_TERMS_URL and VITE_PRIVACY_URL name, and that site logs
    the visit like any website; when that site is hosted for Cyra, its request logs are
    covered by the Product interaction entry too.)
  - Rate limits. The AI and pulse modules keep a salted hash of the client address (the
    salt changes every UTC day) with the times of the last minute's requests, in memory
    only, dropped about a minute (60-65 s) after the last request.
  - No analytics, advertising, crash-reporting or tracking SDK is in the app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  File timestamp APIs (FileAttributeKey.creationDate and .modificationDate).
		  Not called by the App target's own Swift (AppDelegate, SceneDelegate,
		  CyraViewController, CyraHealthPlugin use createDirectory, setAttributes with
		  protectionKey, URL resource values isExcludedFromBackup, fileProtection and
		  isRegularFile, and contentsOfDirectory; none of these is on Apple's required
		  reason list). They are read by the Capacitor Filesystem plugin's library
		  (ion-ios-filesystem IONFILEItemAttributeModel, behind Filesystem.stat), which
		  Swift Package Manager links statically into this App executable. Neither
		  CapacitorFilesystem 8.1.4 nor ion-ios-filesystem ships its own privacy
		  manifest, and Apple requires the bundle containing the executable to report the
		  API. C617.1: timestamps of files inside the app container only.
		  Remove this entry if a future Filesystem release bundles its own manifest.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  Health. Leaves the device only through features the person turns on:
		  - POST /api/pulse/tally ("Share anonymous weekly counts"): allowlisted yes/no flags
		    (hot flashes, night sweats, rough night, cramps, mood swings before a predicted
		    period, heavy day, nausea, kicks, swelling) with the life stage group, at most once
		    a day, with no token or identifier. The backend writes only weekly aggregate counts {week, stage, event,
		    count}.
		  - POST /api/ai/ask ("Also ask Cyra's AI"): the typed question, which can describe her
		    health, sent only when the on-device library has no answer. The backend forwards it
		    verbatim to Anthropic, a third-party processor whose retention is longer than real
		    time (see Other user content below).
		  - Oura: daily temperature, resting heart rate, HRV and sleep rows that the backend
		    fetches from Oura with the token the device sends and returns in the same response,
		    writing nothing.
		  - Terra (Fitbit, Garmin, Whoop): temperature, resting heart rate, HRV and sleep rows
		    that the backend keeps from what Terra pushes to it. They wait in memory, never on
		    disk, in a mailbox keyed by a one-way hash of a secret mailbox key that is kept on her
		    device (and inside any encrypted backup she exports, so a device she restores that
		    backup to holds it too) and that the app sends to the backend only in the body of its
		    own requests, until a device holding that key collects them; a sweep that runs every
		    minute deletes each row no later than 7 days after it first arrived.
		  Linked: the hash that keys the mailbox is Terra's reference id for the person's
		  wearable connection (declared below as a linked User ID), so the Terra rows are linked,
		  and the label is per data type, so Health as a whole is declared linked (Apple: data
		  is unlinked only when direct identifiers such as a user ID are stripped before
		  collection). The app adds no name, email, Cyra account, reference id or device
		  identifier to the counts or the Ask question. The Oura relay carries only her Oura access
		  token (or, to renew it, her refresh token), which identifies her Oura account to Oura, and
		  the date range; the backend uses them for that one request and stores neither.
		  Purpose: app functionality only. The counts are shown back in the app, and the answers
		  and rows are shown to her. No server-side personalization, analytics, advertising or
		  tracking.
		  Depends on product behavior: if the Terra mailbox goes away, Health could return to
		  not linked.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Sensitive info: Apple lists pregnancy information here. Two paths, both opt-in.
		  (1) POST /api/pulse/tally ("Share anonymous weekly counts"): the stage "preg" and the
		  pregnancy flags (nausea, kicks, swelling), sent at most once a day with no token or
		  identifier; the body is exactly {stage, events} (the backend refuses any other field)
		  and the backend keeps only aggregate weekly counts {week, stage, event, count}.
		  (2) POST /api/ai/ask ("Also ask Cyra's AI"): a question she types can describe her
		  pregnancy; the backend forwards it verbatim to Anthropic, which keeps it under its own
		  terms, and nothing strips her name or contact details from it. The weekly counts read
		  and the Ask request send no stage field.
		  Linked: the tally itself is unlinked, but the label is per data type and Ask text can
		  identify her (see Other user content), so Sensitive info as a whole is declared linked.
		  Same purpose as Health.
		  Could return to not linked only if Ask stops sending free text off the device.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeSensitiveInfo</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Other user content: the question typed into Ask Cyra (up to 500 characters). It is
		  sent only when the person has turned on "Also ask Cyra's AI" on this device and the
		  on-device evidence library has no answer: POST /api/ai/ask {question}, with no stage
		  or other field. The backend forwards the text verbatim to Anthropic, which keeps it
		  under its own terms.
		  Linked: free text can contain anything she types, including her name or contact
		  details, and nothing strips identifying details before it is sent.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Email address and name: optional social sign-in (Apple, Google, Facebook) through
		  /api/oauth. Of the provider's answer, the backend passes on only the name and, when
		  Apple or Google says it is verified, the email (Facebook's email is never passed on).
		  It holds them in memory under a one-time code until this device collects them, and a
		  timer deletes them 5 minutes after the callback whether or not they were collected.
		  No account record is kept. Declared because the hand-off outlives a single request.
		  They identify the person, so they are linked. Anonymous Mode and email registration
		  send neither.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  User ID: the Terra reference id of a Fitbit, Garmin or Whoop connection. The device
		  makes a secret mailbox key (32 random bytes, src/lib/wearables.js newKey), keeps it,
		  and sends it only in request bodies. The backend derives the reference id from it
		  with a one-way hash, registers that with Terra as reference_id and files Terra's
		  deliveries under it (see Health above); the key itself is used only for the request.
		  Terra keeps the reference id with the person's wearable connection until it is
		  disconnected, so it is declared linked.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Other data types: Oura access and refresh tokens. After someone connects Oura, the
		  backend holds the tokens in memory under a one-time code until this device collects
		  them, and a timer deletes them 5 minutes after the callback either way
		  (integrations/index.js, handoffs). After that the device keeps them and sends them
		  with each pull, refresh or disconnect, and the backend uses them for that request
		  only. Apple has no credentials category; Apple says a token used on a server call and
		  not retained need not be disclosed, but this hand-off outlives a single request, the
		  same reason Email and Name are declared. They open the person's Oura account, so they
		  are linked.
		  Depends on product behavior: if the hand-off stops holding tokens on the server,
		  this entry can go.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Product interaction: for each request to the backend, the hosting provider (Render, on
		  Professional workspaces and above) keeps an HTTP request log entry: method, host,
		  requested URL including any query string, status, response time, a request ID, the
		  client IP address and (where Render records it) the user agent. That includes the
		  automatic weekly-counts read at launch, the Terra mailbox collection at launch and on
		  return to the foreground, and the opt-in tally and Ask calls; and, when the Terms or
		  Privacy Policy pages are hosted for Cyra, each visit to them. Requests the app sends
		  itself carry no identifier in the URL; secrets and keys travel only in request bodies,
		  which Render does not log. Some URLs carry third-party parameters: the Oura callback
		  (Oura's one-time code, used once and discarded), the sign-in and Oura start pages (the
		  return link and the app challenge, neither a secret), and the Terra return page, to
		  which Terra appends its user_id and the connection's reference_id (see User ID). That
		  return page can tie a log entry to the reference id, so this entry is declared linked.
		  Retention is set by the hosting plan. Cyra does not export these logs or join them to
		  any other data.
		  Could return to not linked only if Terra's return no longer passes through a logged
		  backend URL and Render confirms query strings are not recorded.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
